## Core Development Principles

### 1. Code Quality & Standards
- **TypeScript First**: All code MUST be written in TypeScript with strict type checking
- **No `any` types**: Use proper type definitions, interfaces, and generics
- **Consistent naming**: Use camelCase for variables/functions, PascalCase for classes/interfaces
- **Error handling**: Always implement proper error handling with try-catch blocks
- **Input validation**: Validate all user inputs at controller level
- **Security**: Never expose sensitive data, always sanitize inputs

### 2. Architecture Patterns
- **MVC Pattern**: Controllers handle requests, Services contain business logic
- **Dependency Injection**: Use constructor injection for services
- **Single Responsibility**: Each class/function should have one clear purpose
- **Interface Segregation**: Define clear interfaces for all service contracts
- **Repository Pattern**: Use Prisma as data access layer with proper abstractions

### 3. Database & ORM Best Practices
- **Prisma ORM**: Use Prisma for all database operations
- **Type Safety**: Leverage Prisma's generated types
- **Transactions**: Use database transactions for multi-step operations
- **Migrations**: Always use Prisma migrations for schema changes
- **Indexing**: Add proper database indexes for performance

### 4. API Design Standards
- **RESTful APIs**: Follow REST conventions for all endpoints
- **HTTP Status Codes**: Use appropriate status codes (200, 201, 400, 401, 404, 500)
- **Consistent Response Format**: 
  ```typescript
  {
    message: string,
    data?: any,
    error?: string,
    meta?: { page?, limit?, total? }
  }
  ```
- **Authentication**: JWT-based auth with Bearer tokens
- **CORS**: Proper CORS configuration for production domains

### 5. File Structure & Organization
- **Feature-based grouping**: Group related files by feature/domain
- **Clear separation**: Controllers, Services, Routes, Middlewares in separate directories
- **Index files**: Use index.ts files for clean imports
- **Constants**: Define all constants in dedicated files
- **Environment**: Use .env files for configuration

## Implementation Guidelines

### Controllers
```typescript
// ✅ Good Controller Pattern
export class AdminController {
  private adminService: AdminService;
  
  constructor(adminService: AdminService) {
    this.adminService = adminService;
  }

  async login(req: Request, res: Response): Promise<void> {
    try {
      const { username, password } = req.body;
      
      // Input validation
      if (!username || !password) {
        res.status(400).json({ 
          error: 'Username and password are required' 
        });
        return;
      }

      const result = await this.adminService.login({ username, password });
      
      res.status(200).json({
        message: 'Login successful',
        data: result
      });
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Login error:', error);
      res.status(401).json({ 
        error: 'Invalid credentials' 
      });
    }
  }
}
```

### Services
```typescript
// ✅ Good Service Pattern
export interface IAdminService {
  login(data: AdminLoginData): Promise<LoginResult>;
  createAdmin(data: CreateAdminData): Promise<Admin>;
  verifyToken(token: string): Promise<AdminProfile>;
}

export class AdminService implements IAdminService {
  private readonly jwtSecret: string;
  
  constructor() {
    this.jwtSecret = process.env.JWT_SECRET || 'default-secret';
  }

  async login(data: AdminLoginData): Promise<LoginResult> {
    // Business logic here
  }
}
```

### Error Handling
```typescript
// ✅ Custom Error Classes
export class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(message: string) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'ValidationError';
  }
}

export class AuthenticationError extends Error {
  constructor(message: string = 'Authentication failed') {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'AuthenticationError';
  }
}

// ✅ Global Error Handler
app.use((err: Error, req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {
  console.error('Error:', err);
  
  if (err instanceof ValidationError) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: err.message });
  }
  
  if (err instanceof AuthenticationError) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: err.message });
  }
  
  res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });
});
```

### Database Operations
```typescript
// ✅ Proper Prisma Usage
export class CategoryService {
  async createCategory(data: CreateCategoryData): Promise<Category> {
    return await prisma.category.create({
      data: {
        title: data.title,
        description: data.description,
        imageUrl: data.imageUrl
      },
      include: {
        works: true
      }
    });
  }

  async getCategoriesWithPagination(page: number, limit: number) {
    const skip = (page - 1) * limit;
    
    const [categories, total] = await Promise.all([
      prisma.category.findMany({
        skip,
        take: limit,
        include: {
          works: {
            select: {
              id: true,
              title: true,
              mainImageUrl: true
            }
          }
        },
        orderBy: {
          createdAt: 'desc'
        }
      }),
      prisma.category.count()
    ]);

    return {
      data: categories,
      meta: {
        page,
        limit,
        total,
        totalPages: Math.ceil(total / limit)
      }
    };
  }
}
```

## Security Best Practices

### Authentication & Authorization
- Use JWT tokens with appropriate expiration times
- Implement refresh token mechanism for long-lived sessions
- Hash passwords with bcrypt (minimum 12 rounds)
- Validate and sanitize all inputs
- Use HTTPS in production
- Implement rate limiting for authentication endpoints

### Data Protection
- Never log sensitive information (passwords, tokens)
- Use environment variables for secrets
- Implement proper CORS policies
- Validate file uploads (type, size, content)
- Use parameterized queries (Prisma handles this)

## Performance Guidelines

### Database Optimization
- Use database indexes for frequently queried fields
- Implement pagination for list endpoints
- Use `select` and `include` strategically in Prisma queries
- Avoid N+1 query problems
- Use database transactions for consistency

### API Performance
- Implement response caching where appropriate
- Use compression middleware
- Optimize image uploads and serving
- Implement proper logging and monitoring

## Testing Standards

### Unit Tests
```typescript
// ✅ Service Testing Example
describe('AdminService', () => {
  let adminService: AdminService;
  
  beforeEach(() => {
    adminService = new AdminService();
  });

  it('should create admin with hashed password', async () => {
    const adminData = {
      username: 'testuser',
      password: 'testpass123',
      email: 'test@example.com'
    };

    const admin = await adminService.createAdmin(adminData);
    
    expect(admin.username).toBe(adminData.username);
    expect(admin.passwordHash).not.toBe(adminData.password);
    expect(admin.email).toBe(adminData.email);
  });
});
```

### Integration Tests
- Test API endpoints with real database
- Test authentication flows
- Test file upload functionality
- Test error scenarios

## Deployment & DevOps

### Environment Configuration
```typescript
// ✅ Environment Validation
const requiredEnvVars = [
  'DATABASE_URL',
  'JWT_SECRET',
  'PORT',
  'NODE_ENV'
];

requiredEnvVars.forEach(envVar => {
  if (!process.env[envVar]) {
    throw new Error(`Missing required environment variable: ${envVar}`);
  }
});
```

### Docker Best Practices
- Use multi-stage builds
- Run as non-root user
- Minimize image size
- Use .dockerignore
- Health checks for containers

